@section technical_methods Technical Documentation

@subsection catenary_math Catenary Sag and Analytical Cable Geometry

A ski-lift cable sags as a hyperbolic cosine. In `ice_cable_geometry.py` each span is

@image html Technical_cablesag.png "Cable sag between the lift towers. Arrows mark the lowered centre of each span"

@f[
  z(x) = z_{\text{mid}} + a \left( \cosh\left( \frac{x - x_m}{a} \right) - 1 \right)
@f]

with @f$ a \approx 320\,\text{m} @f$. The bare cable radius is about 0.17 m. A cluster is accepted as ice when its 75th-percentile radial distance from the cable centreline satisfies

@f[
  0.20\,\text{m} \le r_{75} \le 1.50\,\text{m}.
@f]

The upper bound rejects terrain, sky, and large blue markers.

@subsection unified_detour_sec Kinematic-Aware Swept-Volume Detour & Dual-Threshold Cost map Fusion 

Two failure modes appear if planning and steering are treated separately. Inflation cells in a narrow corridor look like walls, so the robot freezes. A point-mass planner then lets the Husky pivot in place, and the corners of its 0.99 m by 0.67 m body strike the rock. We treat both in one five-stage loop.

@dot
digraph DetourKinematicFusion {
  rankdir=TB;
  fontname="Helvetica";
  fontsize=10;
  node [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, shape=box, style="filled,rounded", penwidth=1.1];
  edge [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=8, penwidth=1.1];

  c_in [label="Nav2 Costmap2D & OccupancyGrid", fillcolor="#e7f5ff", color="#1864ab"];

  subgraph cluster_stage1 {
    label="Stage 1: Dual-Threshold Costmap Sieve";
    style="filled"; fillcolor="#f8f9fa"; color="#495057";
    s_sieve [label="Lethal: [99..100] and [253..254]\nInflation halos stay permeable", fillcolor="#fff3bf", color="#f08c00"];
  }

  subgraph cluster_stage2 {
    label="Stage 2: Corridor Slice";
    style="filled"; fillcolor="#f8f9fa"; color="#495057";
    s_slice [label="Sample southern pass lines\nScore toward the goal", fillcolor="#d3f9d8", color="#2b8a3e"];
  }

  subgraph cluster_stage3 {
    label="Stage 3: Swept-Volume Check";
    style="filled"; fillcolor="#f8f9fa"; color="#495057";
    s_dec [label="Obstacle within 1.3 m of the turn?", shape=diamond, fillcolor="#ffc9c9", color="#c92a2a"];
  }

  subgraph cluster_stage4 {
    label="Stage 4: Reverse, then Pivot";
    style="filled"; fillcolor="#f8f9fa"; color="#495057";
    s_rev [label="Back up about 0.5 m\nthen yaw in clear space", fillcolor="#ffe8cc", color="#d9480f"];
  }

  subgraph cluster_stage5 {
    label="Stage 5: Line of Sight";
    style="filled"; fillcolor="#f8f9fa"; color="#495057";
    s_opt [label="Ray-cast to the cable pin\nbefore starting the 5-frame vote", fillcolor="#d0ebff", color="#1971c2"];
  }

  c_in -> s_sieve -> s_slice -> s_dec;
  s_dec -> s_rev [label="yes"];
  s_dec -> s_opt [label="no"];
  s_rev -> s_opt;
}
@enddot

Wheel planning may cross inflation. The camera ray may not: only lethal cells block the sightline. If a rock is inside 1.3 m, the robot reverses at 0.15 m/s (at most 1.8 s) and only then pivots.

@subsection camera_reprojection Slope and altitude

The Husky filter is planar, so on the 4° ramp the assumed camera is too low and the region of interest misses the cable. We read roll, pitch, and height from 3D odometry and project the pin with that pose. Nav2 also commands no vertical speed, so `terrain_altitude_follow.py` adds

@f[
  v_z = \mathrm{clip}\big(K_p\,(z_{\text{terrain}} - z),\; -v_{z,\max},\; v_{z,\max}\big), \quad K_p = 1.5.
@f]

@subsection parameters_table Parameters we tuned

| Parameter | Value | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| HSV hue / saturation / value | 80–125 / at least 28 / at least 90 | Keep cyan ice; drop snow and dark cable |
| Minimum bulge radius | 0.20 m | Above the bare cable |
| Frames / positives required | 5 / 4 | Reject a single-frame false positive |
| Standoff / drive step | 16 m / 4 m | Put the cable in the raised camera; keep goals local |
| Reverse distance | 0.5 m | Open space for the chassis to turn |
| Ground-filter height | 0.20 m | Drop the snow ramp; keep rocks |
| Altitude gain | 1.5 | Climb the ramp with Nav2 |

@subsection tech_limitations Limits we still carry

- Open snow has few LiDAR features, so scan matching drifts until a tower or rock enters view.
- The simulator casts diffuse LiDAR rays. Real glaze ice reflects them away.
- Heavy fog would wash out the colour cue. A thermal camera would be the next sensor.
- Ground confirmation assumes the cable height is known. A broken span would need a second view.



@section operator_interface Operator Interface

The operator Graphical User Interface (GUI) was developed using Python and PySide6 to provide ski resort maintenance personnel with a centralised platform for monitoring autonomous robotic inspections. The interface was designed to improve situational awareness by presenting robot locations, hazard information, camera feeds and mission progress within a single application. The dashboard (`fde_gui`) uses a PyVista view of the same terrain, towers, and cables as Gazebo. Each accepted hazard is written to SQLite with its pose, time, and a link to the evidence image, and the gate will not release an inspection until the operator approves it.

@image html image1.png "Operator interface from the first layout, through the design mock-up, to the implemented GUI"
@image html image2.png "Map legend with hazard markers on the cable, robot poses, and home points"
@image html image3.png "Documented hazard record, including the drone image of a confirmed ice sleeve"
@image html image4.png "Live operations view with both camera streams and the robot action log"

